A||||5||Eric_PA||V6 Fuel pump question||||1||
Z||000000||Eric_PA||08-03-2000||05:09 PM||||Hi, I was wondering if it is normal for the fuel pump to stay running the whole time the ignition is on? Or is it designed to shut off when at pressure. This is without the car running. Also is there an accessory key position. I have two fieros, and according the the schematics there is such a position, but I can't find it on either one. As far as the fuel pump, it's on an 86.5 gt with auto.<BR>Thanks in advance, <BR>Eric||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||08-03-2000||06:45 PM||||The fuel pump is supposed to come on when:<P>1)Oil pressure sender detects (almost) any oil pressure - whether ignition is on or off.<P>2)ECM says so.<P>I do <B>not</B> think the fuel pump is supposed to run continuously just because ignition is turned on.<P>And yes, all my Fieros seem to have an accessory position for the ignition switch.  Believe I might have to push key inward slightly to get it to turn to ACC position.<P>     ||||reg||4
Z||000002||DRH||08-03-2000||07:05 PM||||Pretty much what StuGood said. <P>I'll add that the ECM will turn the fuel pump on for about 2 seconds when the key is first turned to ON.<P>The ACC position is all the way back (counter clockwise) past the point where the steering wheel locks and the key will come out. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||08-05-2000||06:45 PM||||My fuel pump ran all the time and It was the oil pressure sending switch. I should have known because the oil pressure gauge was acting funny. Replaced the sensor and everything worked fine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ka4nkf||08-05-2000||08:20 PM||||I forgot to mention. If you disconnect the connector going to the oil pressure sender, and the fuel pump stops running then you have found your problem. otherwise if It dont quit running then you will have to do some more checking. hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric_PA||08-06-2000||07:59 PM||||Thanks for the replies so far guys. I originally had problems with the oil pressure gauge. I did change the pressure switch, but not for the fuel pump problem. When I first got the car, I would have to crank it forever to wait for the pressure switch to make before the car would start. I found the problem to be the relay from the ecm that controls the fuel pump. Acording to the electrical diagram the oil pressure switch creates what is called a latch once the car is started to keep the contacts closed on the relay. I replaced a relay that is controled by the ecm, now it starts right up, but the fuel pump never stops running. Not good if I would actually lose oil pressure. I'm starting to think that the used relay that I put in is bad, and is always closed. I'm going to check it tonight. ALso will check the new oil pressure switch that I put in, you never know, new things some times have defects.<P>As for the key turned to the accessory point, I tried everything to get it there. I think that it is broken on both of my fieros. <P>Eric<BR>||||reg||1
